WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.858 --> 00:00:03.935
A requirement is what the new system must do or be,

2
00:00:04.062 --> 00:00:05.862
written so you can check it later.

3
00:00:06.909 --> 00:00:10.346
It is the basis for the quote, the tests and acceptance.

4
00:00:11.349 --> 00:00:14.322
A functional requirement says what the system does:

5
00:00:14.496 --> 00:00:17.015
it blocks a batch quality has not released.

6
00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:21.158
A non-functional requirement says how well: speed,

7
00:00:21.367 --> 00:00:25.326
availability, security, like a label within three seconds.

8
00:00:26.629 --> 00:00:30.577
Start from the process, not the screens: first the to-be process,

9
00:00:30.692 --> 00:00:33.792
how the work runs after go-live, step by step.

10
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.817
Then ask, per step, what the system must do.

11
00:00:38.851 --> 00:00:43.553
Start from the old system and you get a wish list: the old system in new colours,

12
00:00:43.681 --> 00:00:44.621
detours and all.

13
00:00:45.608 --> 00:00:47.535
A good requirement is testable.

14
00:00:48.253 --> 00:00:51.689
User-friendly is not: nobody can say pass or fail.

15
00:00:52.709 --> 00:00:54.706
This is: after an hour's training,

16
00:00:54.892 --> 00:00:58.630
a new operator logs a line stop in under thirty seconds.

17
00:00:59.625 --> 00:01:03.444
Give every line an ID, a source and later a priority.

18
00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:07.381
The ID returns in test scripts and contract.

19
00:01:08.789 --> 00:01:10.809
Write them with the people who know the work,

20
00:01:11.018 --> 00:01:14.838
and a business analyst who translates between business and IT.

21
00:01:15.849 --> 00:01:18.984
Watch out for solutions disguised as requirements,

22
00:01:19.146 --> 00:01:21.410
like an Excel export of all orders.

23
00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:27.639
The real need may be: the planner sees tomorrow's orders per line, with materials.

24
00:01:28.645 --> 00:01:33.173
What often goes wrong: two big workshops, no check against the to-be process,

25
00:01:33.393 --> 00:01:35.936
and a quote on three hundred vague lines.

26
00:01:36.957 --> 00:01:40.417
Every vague line later becomes an argument: was it in scope?

27
00:01:41.483 --> 00:01:43.817
Ask: which step or decision gets better?

28
00:01:44.578 --> 00:01:47.852
If nobody knows, it is probably habit, not need.

29
00:01:49.196 --> 00:01:54.896
A meat products plant replaces its MES, the system that runs orders on the floor.

30
00:01:55.609 --> 00:01:58.558
Three workshops: two hundred and forty wishes.

31
00:01:59.672 --> 00:02:03.654
Step one: sixty duplicates out, a hundred and eighty left.

32
00:02:04.753 --> 00:02:07.656
Step two: the Excel export becomes a need.

33
00:02:08.672 --> 00:02:12.701
Step three: fast labels become testable: within three seconds,

34
00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:15.023
in ninety-five of a hundred tests.

35
00:02:16.016 --> 00:02:20.346
Step four: thirty-six lines serve no process step, and are parked.

36
00:02:21.417 --> 00:02:27.129
Result: a hundred and forty-four requirements, each with an ID, a source and a test.

37
00:02:28.461 --> 00:02:31.758
MoSCoW sorts requirements into four bins: Must have,

38
00:02:31.921 --> 00:02:34.011
Should have, Could have and Won’t have this time.

39
00:02:34.992 --> 00:02:37.035
The o's only make it pronounceable.

40
00:02:38.027 --> 00:02:41.510
Dai Clegg devised it in nineteen ninety-four at Oracle,

41
00:02:41.603 --> 00:02:43.925
for development projects with little time.

42
00:02:44.937 --> 00:02:47.723
It later became a fixed part of DSDM,

43
00:02:47.897 --> 00:02:51.531
an agile method now kept by the Agile Business Consortium.

44
00:02:52.544 --> 00:02:57.652
In ERP and MES projects it is the most common way to prioritise.

45
00:02:58.953 --> 00:03:02.424
A Must have is something without which go-live makes no sense,

46
00:03:02.587 --> 00:03:04.189
or is unsafe or illegal.

47
00:03:04.854 --> 00:03:08.174
DSDM calls the Musts the Minimum Usable SubseT.

48
00:03:09.261 --> 00:03:12.663
The test question: without this, do we postpone go-live?

49
00:03:13.412 --> 00:03:15.932
If we'd work around it, it is not a Must.

50
00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:20.858
A Should have is important, but a workaround is possible for a while.

51
00:03:21.875 --> 00:03:25.822
A Could have is nice to have, and goes first when time runs short.

52
00:03:27.227 --> 00:03:30.536
Won’t have this time means: agreed not to deliver it now.

53
00:03:31.538 --> 00:03:35.694
It still goes on the list, so it can't creep back in through the side door.

54
00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:41.541
DSDM's rules of thumb: Musts typically no more than sixty percent of the effort,

55
00:03:41.716 --> 00:03:43.005
Coulds around twenty.

56
00:03:43.976 --> 00:03:45.427
The Coulds are your reserve.

57
00:03:46.478 --> 00:03:47.894
If everything is a Must,

58
00:03:47.999 --> 00:03:52.016
the scope cannot absorb a setback: the date or the budget breaks.

59
00:03:53.306 --> 00:03:58.414
MVP: minimum viable product, Frank Robinson's term from 2001.

60
00:03:59.414 --> 00:04:02.131
Eric Ries made it famous with The Lean Startup:

61
00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:05.776
the smallest version to learn the most from real users.

62
00:04:06.791 --> 00:04:12.236
In ERP and MES it is looser: the smallest scope to go live on a real line.

63
00:04:12.926 --> 00:04:16.432
All above the MVP cut line must be ready for go-live.

64
00:04:17.487 --> 00:04:22.712
The whole process, thin but complete: order in, produce, label, ship, invoice.

65
00:04:23.911 --> 00:04:26.964
Half the screens of every step is not an MVP.

66
00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:31.761
Familiar to a Lean consultant: start small, on one line.

67
00:04:32.452 --> 00:04:37.375
But legal and safety Musts, such as tracing food batches, are never trimmed.

68
00:04:38.406 --> 00:04:41.308
Ask: what do we do on the first Monday without this?

69
00:04:42.624 --> 00:04:45.689
An injection moulder introduces an MES:

70
00:04:45.805 --> 00:04:49.660
three hundred person-days of build capacity until go-live.

71
00:04:50.642 --> 00:04:56.145
Forty requirements: Musts two hundred and ten days, Shoulds thirty, Coulds sixty.

72
00:04:57.005 --> 00:04:59.234
Exactly three hundred, no room.

73
00:05:00.247 --> 00:05:04.264
Step one: Musts are seventy percent, above DSDM's sixty.

74
00:05:05.229 --> 00:05:07.121
Step two: the test question.

75
00:05:07.831 --> 00:05:11.604
Six requirements, forty-five days, have a workaround,

76
00:05:11.732 --> 00:05:13.764
such as a weekly report by hand.

77
00:05:14.581 --> 00:05:15.579
They move to Should.

78
00:05:16.668 --> 00:05:21.312
Step three: Musts a hundred and sixty-five days, fifty-five percent.

79
00:05:22.067 --> 00:05:24.354
Shoulds seventy-five, Coulds sixty.

80
00:05:25.382 --> 00:05:29.852
The MVP cut line sits under the Musts: order to shipment on line one.
